<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indus Valley Civilisation</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> Civilisation was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> civilisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> basin, known for planned cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a>. It is generally identified with the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meluhha">Meluhha</a> in Mesopotamian texts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> traded with it by sea through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation.jpg" alt="Indus Valley Civilisation" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indus_Valley_Civilization,_Mature_Phase_(2600-1900_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The civilisation lasted from 3300 BCE to 1300 BCE, and in its mature form from 2600 BCE to 1900 BCE, across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Its area was larger than ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> combined. It is called Harappan after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a>, the first site excavated, in the 1920s. The early Harappan cultures grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> cultures, of which the best known is at Mehrgarh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a>, one of the earliest sites of farming and herding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. A gradual drying of the region during the third millennium BCE may have stimulated urbanisation, and flood-supported farming produced surpluses that supported cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Over a thousand Mature Harappan sites have been reported, but there are only five major urban centres: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a>, Ganeriwala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dholavira">Dholavira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakhigarhi">Rakhigarhi</a>. Mohenjo-daro and Harappa probably held 30,000 to 60,000 people each. The cities were noted for grid planning, baked-brick houses, covered drains and water supply, and merchants traded by caravan with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>.</p><p>Around 1900 BCE signs of decline appeared, and by about 1700 BCE most cities were abandoned. In 1953 Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mortimer-wheeler">Mortimer Wheeler</a> proposed that Aryan invaders caused the end, but scholars rejected this because the skeletons he cited belonged to a later period. Many now point to drought, a weakening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> and a decline in trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. After 1900 BCE the number of sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> rose from 218 to 853, as people moved east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Archaeology gives no direct answer about who ruled the cities. The consistency of weights and measures and the uniform planning suggest some central authority, and theories range from a single state to self-governing city states. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> scale from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothal">Lothal</a> has a smallest division of about 1.704 millimetres. Craftsmen worked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and bronze alloyed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, arsenic and lead. Between 400 and 600 distinct symbols of the undeciphered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-script">Indus script</a> are known, and typical inscriptions are about five characters long. Interpretations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> religion are largely speculative. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a> was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1980 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dholavira">Dholavira</a> in 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indus Valley Civilisation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Akkadian Empire governed one of the most economically productive regions of the ancient world, the alluvial plain of southern Mesopotamia fed by the Tigris and Euphrates, and connected it through trade networks reaching from the <b>Indus Valley Civilisation</b> to the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Indus plain held Mehrgarh, the cities of the <b>Indus Valley Civilisation</b> and Gandhara, and official chronology dates the foundation of Pakistan to Muhammad ibn Qasim's conquest of Sindh in 711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Whether elements of it go back to the cities of the <b>Indus Valley civilisation</b>, as some scholars have suggested from its seals and figurines, remains uncertain.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The religion then developed in the northwest during the early Vedic period (c. 1500–1100 BCE), influenced by the remnants of the late <b>Indus Valley Civilisation</b>; White (2003) cites three scholars who have emphatically demonstrated that it partly derives from that civilisation.</blockquote><a href="/religion/vedic-religion">Vedic religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>This may have happened during, or in the century before, the global drought credited with bringing down early civilisations such as the <b>Indus Valley Civilisation</b>, Old Kingdom Egypt and the Akkadian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mehrgarh’s long occupation ended in the time of the <b>Indus Valley Civilisation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mehrgarh-culture">Mehrgarh culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
